**Acceptance checks**

- Twenty-four simultaneously connected clients share one daemon process, one provider object, at most one provider stream, and one recovery poll scheduler.
- All 24 receive initial replay and a later authoritative update; one paused reader cannot delay the other 23.
- Concurrent first-use launch attempts result in one listener/provider; losers connect to it or exit.
- A killed daemon causes clients to enter reconnecting state, settle pending calls with connection-lost/indeterminate errors, start one replacement, and accept its replay. No command is automatically duplicated.
- Stale socket/start-marker recovery never unlinks a foreign-owned, non-socket, or symlink path.
- Queue/frame/in-flight overflow produces stable typed errors or client isolation rather than unbounded memory.
- Closing all clients allows the daemon and provider process to exit after the configured grace period; clients do not keep OpenCode/Pi alive after disposal.

